one of the interesting things about the  multiverse is that it raises questions  about probabilities that we still don't  really quite know how to answer the  issue is that everything that is allowed  by the laws of physics happens in an  infinite multiverse an infinite number  of times so for example you might want  to compare the number of births of  one-headed cows with the number of  births of two-headed cows and you'd  naturally think that there are a lot  more one-headed cows than two-headed  cows but if there's an infinite number  of both one really needs some way of  regulating things in order to be able to  talk about what's the ratio of  two-headed cows to one head cows the  current status of things is that we can  devise ways of regularizing these  probabilities of turning the infinities  into finite ratios but they're different  ways of doing it there's no unique  solution and it even turns out that some  of the simple things that you might try  turn out to give results that are very  crazy sounding so we think that we have  narrowed down a range of possible  prescriptions for defining probabilities  but we still don't know the answer to  what is it that actually determines what  are the correct probabilities in the  context of the multiverse
 

